Application Health Probes 
Objectives Describe how Kubernetes uses health probes during deployment, scaling, and failover of applications.

1. What Are Health Probes?
Health probes are mechanisms Kubernetes uses to check the status of a pod/container. They ensure:
•	Crashing pods are restarted automatically.
•	Traffic only goes to healthy pods.
•	Scaling decisions happen only when new pods are ready.
•	Failures are monitored effectively.
Think of probes as heartbeat checks for your application.
________________________________________
2. Types of Probes
Kubernetes supports three main types of probes:
a) Startup Probe
•	Checks if an application has finished starting.
•	Only runs until the app reports it’s started.
•	If the probe fails beyond a timeout, the pod restarts (restartPolicy applies).
•	Useful for apps with long startup times.
Example YAML snippet:
startupProbe:
  httpGet:
    path: /health
    port: 3000
  periodSeconds: 10
  failureThreshold: 12
•	Runs every 10 seconds, allows 12 failures before restarting.
•	Total wait = 12 × 10 = 120 seconds.
________________________________________
b) Readiness Probe
•	Checks if the pod is ready to serve traffic.
•	If it fails, Kubernetes removes pod from service endpoints.
•	Detects temporary unavailability (like batch jobs or slow initialization).
Example YAML snippet:
readinessProbe:
  httpGet:
    path: /ready
    port: 3000
  periodSeconds: 5
  failureThreshold: 3
•	Runs every 5 seconds, fails after 3 consecutive failures.
•	If successful again, pod is added back to service.
________________________________________
c) Liveness Probe
•	Checks if the application is alive.
•	If it fails enough times, pod is restarted.
•	Runs throughout the pod’s lifetime (after startup completes).
Example YAML snippet:
livenessProbe:
  httpGet:
    path: /health
    port: 3000
  periodSeconds: 10
  failureThreshold: 6
•	Runs every 10 seconds, restarts pod if probe fails 6 consecutive times.
________________________________________
3. Types of Tests in Probes
You can define probes using three methods:
Type	How it works
HTTP GET	Sends an HTTP GET request to a pod endpoint. 200–399 = success, others = failure.
Command	Runs a shell command inside the container. Exit 0 = success, anything else = failure.
TCP Socket	Tries to open a TCP socket on a port. Connection = success.
Example using command:
livenessProbe:
  exec:
    command:
      - cat
      - /tmp/healthy
  periodSeconds: 5
  failureThreshold: 3
________________________________________
4. Probe Timing Variables
•	periodSeconds → how often the probe runs.
•	failureThreshold → how many consecutive failures before the pod is considered unhealthy.
•	Example: periodSeconds: 5, failureThreshold: 3 → pod has 15 seconds to recover before mitigation.
Key: Too frequent probes waste resources; too infrequent slows response to failures.
________________________________________
5. Adding Probes in YAML
You can define probes inside a pod template (deployment, statefulset):
Deployment example:
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: web-server
spec:
  replicas: 3
  template:
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: web-server
        image: my-web-app:v1
        livenessProbe:
          httpGet:
            path: /health
            port: 3000
          periodSeconds: 10
          failureThreshold: 6
        readinessProbe:
          httpGet:
            path: /ready
            port: 3000
          periodSeconds: 5
          failureThreshold: 3
•	Liveness → restarts container if unhealthy.
•	Readiness → temporarily removes pod from service if not ready.
________________________________________
6. Adding Probes via CLI
You can also add or modify probes without editing YAML using oc set probe (OpenShift only):
oc set probe deployment/front-end \
--readiness \
--failure-threshold=6 \
--period-seconds=10 \
--get-url=http://:8080/healthz
•	Adds a readiness probe to the front-end deployment.
•	You can also use --liveness or --startup.
________________________________________
7. Adding Probes via Web Console
1.	Navigate to Workloads → Deployments.
2.	Select the deployment.
3.	Click Actions → Add Health Checks.
4.	Edit the probe: set type, path, port, headers, etc.
Useful if you prefer GUI over YAML or CLI.
________________________________________
8. Key Takeaways
•	Startup Probe → ensures pod finishes starting before checking liveness.
•	Readiness Probe → controls routing to pod, handles temporary unavailability.
•	Liveness Probe → restarts unhealthy pods, ensures reliability.
•	HTTP/Command/TCP → flexible probe types depending on your app.
•	Timing → balance period and failureThreshold to avoid wasted resources.
•	Probes work with Deployments to improve scaling, failover, and HA.
